WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.160
 Star Wars fans would be familiar with the golden, life-sized hospitality robot C-3PO.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.880
 While Star Wars might be set in a galaxy far, far away, the reality of having machines

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.040
 talk and respond to us in a human-like manner is already a reality, which keeps getting

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.240
 more and more realistic with every passing day.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.600
 The people you ask for queries on websites, your smart assistants, even calls made over

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.880
 the internet, all of them have one thing in common: none of them are actually human.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.500
 Now, you must be thinking, if they are not human, how do they manage to sound and seem

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.340
 so human-like, how do they respond to me so intelligently, and how are they so articulate?

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.520
 This my friends, is the magic of natural language processing.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.560
 What is NLP?

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.120
 Natural language processing, or NLP, refers to the branch of artificial intelligence that

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.760
 gives the machines the ability to read, understand, and derive meaning from human languages.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.760
 NLP combines the field of linguistics and computer science to decipher language structure and

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.260
 guidelines, and to make models which can comprehend, break down, and separate significant details

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.140
 from text and speech.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.440
 Every day, humans interact with each other through public social media, transferring vast quantities

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.220
 of freely available data to each other.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.540
 This data is extremely useful in understanding human behavior and customer habits.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.200
 Data analysts and machine learning experts utilize this data to give machines the ability

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.180
 to mimic human linguistic behavior.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.260
 This helps save millions in terms of manpower and time, as you don't need to always have

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.760
 a person present at the other end of a phone.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.980
 NLP is also a lot more widespread than you may realize.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.820
 You use it every day in seemingly normal and insignificant situations.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.500
 Don't know how to correctly spell a word?

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.780
 Autocorrect has you covered.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.540
 Need to see if your article or thesis will get flagged for copyright violations?

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.880
 That's okay.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.420
 A plagiarism checker will search through the web and find any cases of published documents,

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.220
 which may match your work line by line.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:07.080
 While NLP seems really cool, yet a cutting-edge and complicated technology concept, it is actually

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.580
 pretty easy to learn.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.980
 You start off with a document or an article.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.220
 To make your algorithm understand what is going on in it, you need to process it into

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.100
 a form which is easily comprehensible by the machine.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.020
 This is no different than making a child learn to read for the first time.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.380
 You start off by performing segmentation, which is to break the entire document down

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.000
 into its constituent sentences.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.320
 You can do this by segmenting the article along its punctuations like full stops and commas.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.900
 For the algorithm to understand these sentences, we get the words in a sentence and to explain

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.180
 them individually to our algorithm.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:47.620
 So we break down our sentence into its constituent words and store them.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.600
 This is called tokenizing, where each word is called a token.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.100
 We can make the learning process faster by getting rid of non-essential words which do not

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.340
 add much meaning to our statement and are just there to make our statement sound more cohesive.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.040
 These words such as "are" and "the" are called stop words.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.720
 Now that we have the basic form of our document, we need to explain it to our machine.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.660
 We first start off by explaining that some words like skipping, skips, skipped are the same

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.040
 word with added prefixes and suffixes.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.720
 This is called stemming.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.900
 We also identify the base words for different word tense, mood, gender, etc.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.660
 This is called lemmatization, stemming from the base word "limma".

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:35.080
 Now we explain the concept of nouns, verbs, articles and other parts of speech to the machine by

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.280
 adding these tags to our words.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.820
 This is called part of speech tagging.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.900
 Next, we introduce our machine to pop culture references and everyday names by flagging names

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.800
 of movies, important personalities or locations, etc. that may occur in the document.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.240
 This is called named entity tagging.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:58.460
 Once we have our base words and tags, we use a machine learning algorithm like Naive Bayes

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.420
 to teach our model humans sentiment and speech.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:06.060
 At the end of the day, most of the techniques used in NLP are simple grammar techniques that

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.060
 we have been taught in school.

